;Match details

* '''Match 1: Maeda def. Morita.''' The Speed Star's rebound. Maeda, 1-1 after the Day 1 loss to Kengo, needed the win and controlled from the opening bell — his speed too much for Morita's honest game. Morita fought with Seigigun heart and nearly stole it with a flash cradle at 2.6, but the Speed Star Press at 12:15 ended it. Maeda is 2-1; Morita is 0-3, and the questions about his seed are getting louder. '''Crowd:''' POP for Maeda. '''MAEDA''' chants. GASPS at the flash cradle. POP for the Speed Star Press. Respectful applause for Morita.

* '''Match 2: Marauder def. Honda.''' The mystery vs. the outsider. Honda, 1 point off the draw with the champion, had the tournament's best claim on momentum — and the Marauder took it apart. The AOPW representative's grappling is honest and technical; the Marauder does not engage with honest. Honda's leg work — the same approach that took a point from Leoni — landed clean and the Marauder did not sell it. At 10 minutes, Honda went for the gutwrench suplex; the Marauder blocked it, lifted, and delivered the power bomb at 10:48. Clean pin. The Marauder is 2-1 and the block's most uncomfortable problem; Honda's momentum has been stopped. '''Crowd:''' Uneasy silence for the Marauder's entrance. POP for Honda. GASPS when the leg work did not land. GASPS for the power bomb. Murmurs — the crowd does not know what it is watching with this man.

* '''Match 3: Nakamura def. Stan.''' The injury match. Stan walked to the ring with the left knee braced, and the Aizawa crowd — which knew him as the man who walked out on Kengo at [[Island Invasion Tour 2026|Island Invasion]] — received him warmly. Nakamura, 0-2, had promised he would not be gentle, and he was not. The plan was visible from the first minute: knees to the injured left leg, a dragon screw, a kneebar on the damaged meniscus. Stan's power still showed — he caught Nakamura's running knee and threw him across the ring at 6 minutes, and the crowd believed. But the knee would not hold. At 10 minutes, Stan attempted the chokeslam — the same move that buckled him in Tokuyama — and the knee gave out on the lift, the giant collapsing in the center of the ring with no one touching him. Nakamura looked down at him. He waited. The referee checked on Stan, who pushed him away and pulled himself up by the ropes — and Nakamura hit the Boma Ye at 11:23. Clean pin. Nakamura's first points of the tournament. Stan stayed down for two minutes. The crowd stood for him as he was helped to the back — the ovation was for the man, not the result. '''Crowd:''' WARM POP for Stan's entrance — the crowd knows the story. '''STAN''' chants through the knee work. Silence when the knee gave out — the loudest silence of the tournament. GASPS for the Boma Ye. STANDING OVATION as Stan was helped out. Nakamura stood in the ring, watching, and for once did not play to the crowd.

* '''Match 4: Vance def. Kawada.''' The semi-main — two undefeated men. The workhorse against the strong-style ace, and the match delivered everything the block had promised: 19 minutes of forearms that sounded like gunshots, suplexes, and fighting spirit. Kawada's forearms staggered Vance for the first time in the tournament — and Vance answered with his own, the Mercian strong style meeting Sanjin Puro head-on. Kawada hit the brainbuster at 14 minutes — Vance kicked out at 2.85. Vance hit the half nelson suplex at 17 minutes — Kawada kicked out at 2.8. The finish: Kawada went for the top-rope brainbuster, the move that had pinned Nakamura on Day 1; Vance slipped out behind him, caught the waistlock, and delivered the Drill Driver at 19:04. Clean pin. Vance is 3-0, alone atop Block A, and still has not spoken since August 5. Kawada's record falls to 2-1 — but the block knows what it watched. '''Crowd:''' BUILD for both entrances — the building was full and loud before a move was hit. Dueling '''VANCE''' / '''KAWADA''' chants. '''THIS IS WRESTLING''' during the strike exchanges. GASPS at both kickouts. ERUPTION for the Drill Driver. Standing ovation. '''SPEAK''' chants as Vance walked out — he did not.

* '''Match 5: Leoni def. Kengo.''' The main event — the family war in the champion's backyard. Kengo walked out first, alone, to the worst heat of the tournament. The Cannon Club is dead; the leader is alone; and the cousin he cannot beat was waiting in the ring where he grew up. Kengo attacked before the bell — the desperation of a man with nothing left — and for five minutes it worked: eye rake, rope grab, the shortcuts of a man who cannot win clean. Leoni absorbed it, and the match turned. The Samoan drop. The running hip attack. The drive-by. Kengo kicked out of the Samoan drop at 2.5 and fought back with a lariat and a power bomb of his own — his best stretch of the tournament — but the gap was the gap. At 15 minutes, Leoni hit the Spear of Fangatapu. Kengo kicked out at 2.4 — the first man to kick out of the Spear this tournament. Leoni looked at him, nodded once — the nod of the chief to a warrior who earned one more — and hit the Namo Strike at 16:52. Clean pin. Leoni did not celebrate. He stood over his cousin, looked down at him, and left the ring without a word. Kengo sat in the ring alone, in the champion's home city, in front of 11,500 people who did not sing for him. '''Crowd:''' NUCLEAR HEAT for Kengo's entrance — the Aizawa crowd knows the family history. NUCLEAR POP for Leoni — the taualga, the pate, the namo, the homecoming. '''LEONI''' chants throughout. BOOS at every shortcut. GASPS when Kengo kicked out of the Spear. ERUPTION for the Namo Strike. Silence after — the crowd understood the moment. The chief had beaten his cousin in his own house, and there was nothing to celebrate. '''Crowd note:''' the silence was not indifference — it was the sound of a family matter ending the only way it could.

